\label{ch:code-emission}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:code-emission-intro}

Once the compiler has selected instructions, allocated registers, and scheduled operations, the final step is to produce a binary artifact that the operating system can load and execute. This chapter covers the production of object files, the resolution of references between compilation units, and the construction of executable programs. These tasks---code emission, relocation, and linking---form the bridge between the compiler's output and the operating system's loader.

The journey from a compiler's internal representation to a runnable executable involves several distinct stages, each with its own format and conventions. The compiler emits an \emph{object file} containing machine code, data, and metadata. The \emph{linker} combines multiple object files and libraries, resolving cross-references and producing an executable or shared library. The \emph{loader} maps the executable into memory and transfers control to its entry point. Understanding this pipeline is essential for any compiler developer.

Part~XXIX separates decisions by when the necessary information becomes
available. An assembler can encode the instruction before it can write
the final value of every field (\cref{ch:ext-281}). A static linker assigns
addresses within an output image (\cref{ch:ext-287,ch:ext-288}), while the
loader assigns mappings in a process and may still have to apply dynamic
relocations (\cref{ch:ext-291,ch:ext-293}). Follow
\cref{ch:ext-302} for a single program traced through these stages.

\section{Object File Formats}\label{sec:object-formats}

\subsection{The Role of Object Files}\label{sec:object-role}

An object file is the compiler's output unit. It contains:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Executable code}: the machine instructions generated by the compiler.
  \item \textbf{Initialized data}: global and static variables with initial values.
  \item \textbf{Uninitialized data (BSS)}: global and static variables that are zero-initialized.
  \item \textbf{Symbol table}: names of functions, global variables, and other entities defined or referenced.
  \item \textbf{Relocation entries}: locations in the code or data that must be adjusted when the final address is known.
  \item \textbf{Debug information}: source-level mappings, type information, and variable locations.
  \item \textbf{Section headers}: metadata describing the layout and attributes of each section.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{ELF: Executable and Linkable Format}\label{sec:elf}

ELF is the dominant object file format on Unix-like systems (Linux, BSD, Solaris). It is used for object files, executables, shared libraries, and core dumps.

\begin{definition}[ELF File Structure]\label{def:elf-structure}
An ELF file consists of:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{ELF header}: magic number, architecture, entry point, and offsets to other structures.
  \item \textbf{Program header table}: describes segments for the loader (present in executables).
  \item \textbf{Sections}: the actual content (code, data, symbols, etc.).
  \item \textbf{Section header table}: describes each section's name, type, size, and offset.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Common ELF sections include:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Common ELF sections}
\label{tab:elf-sections}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Section} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Contents} \\
\hline
\texttt{.text} & \texttt{PROGBITS} & Executable code \\
\texttt{.rodata} & \texttt{PROGBITS} & Read-only data (constants, string literals) \\
\texttt{.data} & \texttt{PROGBITS} & Initialized mutable data \\
\texttt{.bss} & \texttt{NOBITS} & Uninitialized data (zero-filled) \\
\texttt{.symtab} & \texttt{SYMTAB} & Symbol table \\
\texttt{.strtab} & \texttt{STRTAB} & String table for symbol names \\
\texttt{.rel[a].text} & \texttt{REL[A]} & Relocations for \texttt{.text} \\
\texttt{.debug\_info} & \texttt{PROGBITS} & DWARF debug information \\
\texttt{.eh\_frame} & \texttt{PROGBITS} & Exception handling frame data \\
\texttt{.got} & \texttt{PROGBITS} & Global offset table \\
\texttt{.plt} & \texttt{PROGBITS} & Procedure linkage table \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Mach-O Format}\label{sec:mach-o}

Mach-O is the object file format used on macOS and iOS. It is similar in concept to ELF but differs in structure:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Uses \emph{load commands} instead of a program header table.
  \item Supports \emph{fat binaries} containing code for multiple architectures in a single file.
  \item Uses a different relocation format with scattered relocations for efficient page-in.
  \item Two-level namespace: symbols are bound to specific libraries, not globally.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{PE/COFF Format}\label{sec:pe-coff}

The Portable Executable (PE) format, derived from COFF, is used on Windows. Key features include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{DOS stub}: a small DOS program that prints ``This program cannot be run in DOS mode.''
  \item \textbf{PE header}: signature, machine type, and offsets to optional and section headers.
  \item \textbf{Optional header}: entry point, image base, subsystem, and data directories.
  \item \textbf{Sections}: \texttt{.text}, \texttt{.data}, \texttt{.rdata}, \texttt{.rsrc} (resources), etc.
  \item \textbf{Import/Export tables}: for dynamic linking.
  \item \textbf{Relocations}: base relocations for address space layout randomization (ASLR).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{WebAssembly and Other Formats}\label{sec:wasm-format}

Modern platforms introduce new binary formats:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{WebAssembly (Wasm)}: a stack-machine binary format for web browsers and standalone runtimes. Uses a compact binary encoding with sections for types, functions, imports, exports, and code.
  \item \textbf{Java Class Files}: bytecode for the JVM, with a constant pool, method bytecode, and attributes.
  \item \textbf{DEX (Dalvik Executable)}: bytecode for Android's ART runtime.
\end{itemize}

\section{Relocation and Symbol Resolution}\label{sec:relocation}

\subsection{The Relocation Problem}\label{sec:relocation-problem}

When the compiler generates code for a function, it does not know the final addresses of global variables or other functions. It emits \emph{relocation entries} marking the locations that must be patched with final addresses.

\begin{definition}[Relocation Entry]\label{def:reloc-entry}
A \emph{relocation entry} is a record $(offset, symbol, type)$ indicating that the value at byte $offset$ in a section must be modified according to $type$ using the final address of $symbol$.
\end{definition}

Common relocation types include:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Common x86-64 relocation types}
\label{tab:reloc-types}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Formula} & \textbf{Usage} \\
\hline
\texttt{R\_X86\_64\_64} & $S + A$ & Absolute 64-bit address \\
\texttt{R\_X86\_64\_PC32} & $S + A - P$ & 32-bit PC-relative \\
\texttt{R\_X86\_64\_GOTPCREL} & $G + GOT + A - P$ & GOT-relative (for PIC) \\
\texttt{R\_X86\_64\_PLT32} & $L + A - P$ & PLT-relative (function calls) \\
\texttt{R\_X86\_64\_32} & $S + A$ & Absolute 32-bit (zero-extended) \\
\texttt{R\_X86\_64\_32S} & $S + A$ & Absolute 32-bit (sign-extended) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

In these formulas, $S$ is the symbol's final address, $A$ is the addend, $P$ is the place being relocated, $G$ is the offset into the GOT, and $L$ is the PLT entry address.

For the direct local call in \Cref{fig:visual-84}, $P$ is the start
of the displacement field, not the address where execution resumes
after the call. An addend of $-4$ accounts for the field's four bytes.

