\subsection{Symbol Binding and Visibility}\label{sec:symbol-binding}

Symbols have two key attributes:

\begin{definition}[Symbol Binding]\label{def:symbol-binding}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Local}: visible only within the object file. Not exported.
  \item \textbf{Global}: visible to other object files during linking. May be defined or undefined (a reference).
  \item \textbf{Weak}: like global, but may be overridden by a strong definition.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Symbol Visibility]\label{def:symbol-visibility}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Default}: visible to other shared libraries.
  \item \textbf{Hidden}: not visible outside the shared library (but may be used internally).
  \item \textbf{Protected}: visible but not preemptable (the definition in the current shared library is always used).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The compiler controls these attributes through language-level annotations (e.g., \texttt{\_\_attribute\_\_((visibility("hidden")))} in C) and must emit the appropriate symbol table entries.

\section{Static and Dynamic Linking}\label{sec:linking}

\subsection{Static Linking}\label{sec:static-linking}

Static linking combines all object files and libraries into a single executable at build time. The static linker performs:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Symbol resolution}: for each undefined reference, find the defining object file or library.
  \item \textbf{Layout}: assign final addresses to all sections, respecting alignment constraints.
  \item \textbf{Relocation}: patch all relocation entries with final addresses.
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination}: remove unreferenced sections (with \texttt{--gc-sections}).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}[Symbol Resolution Algorithm]\label{def:symbol-resolution}
The linker maintains a global symbol table. For each input object file:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item For each defined global symbol, add it to the table (error on duplicate strong definitions).
  \item For each undefined symbol, record the reference.
\end{enumerate}
After processing all inputs, all undefined references must be resolved. Unresolved references cause a link error.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Dynamic Linking}\label{sec:dynamic-linking}

Dynamic linking defers some linking decisions until load time. Shared libraries (ELF \texttt{.so}, Mach-O \texttt{.dylib}, PE \texttt{.dll}) contain code that can be loaded into multiple processes' address spaces.

\begin{definition}[Dynamic Linking]\label{def:dynamic-linking}
\emph{Dynamic linking} is the process of resolving references to symbols in shared libraries at load time or on first use. The dynamic linker (\texttt{ld-linux.so}, \texttt{dyld}, \texttt{ntdll.dll}) performs this resolution.
\end{definition}

The key mechanisms enabling dynamic linking are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Global Offset Table (GOT)}: a table of addresses used by position-independent code to access global data.
  \item \textbf{Procedure Linkage Table (PLT)}: a table of stubs used to call functions in shared libraries.
  \item \textbf{Lazy binding}: PLT entries are resolved on first call, not at load time.
\end{itemize}

