\subsection{Linker Scripts}\label{sec:linker-scripts}

Linker scripts control the layout of the output file. They specify:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The order and placement of input sections into output sections.
  \item The base address and alignment of segments.
  \item The entry point symbol.
  \item Custom section creation and discarding.
\end{itemize}

The compiler may generate linker scripts (or pass linker flags) to control layout for optimization purposes, such as placing hot code together for improved instruction cache performance.

\section{Position-Independent Code}\label{sec:pic}

\subsection{The Need for Position Independence}\label{sec:pic-need}

Shared libraries must be loadable at any virtual address. This requires that the code not contain absolute addresses that assume a specific load address.

\begin{definition}[Position-Independent Code]\label{def:pic}
\emph{Position-independent code} (PIC) is machine code that executes correctly regardless of its absolute address in memory. All address references are relative (PC-relative or GOT-relative).
\end{definition}

\subsection{PIC Implementation Strategies}\label{sec:pic-implementation}

\subsubsection{x86-64 PIC}

On x86-64, PIC is natural because the architecture provides PC-relative addressing for most operations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Data access: \texttt{mov rax, [rip + symbol@GOTPCREL]} loads the GOT entry.
  \item Function calls: \texttt{call symbol@PLT} uses the PLT stub.
  \item Local data: direct PC-relative addressing without the GOT.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{ARM64 PIC}

ARM64 uses a different strategy:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{ADR} / \texttt{ADRP} instructions compute PC-relative page addresses.
  \item GOT access: \texttt{ADR} to the GOT page, then \texttt{LDR} with the GOT entry offset.
  \item Function calls: direct \texttt{BL} with PC-relative offset (within $\pm 128$MB).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{PIC Costs}\label{sec:pic-costs}

PIC imposes overhead compared to position-dependent code:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Extra instructions for GOT/PLT indirection.
  \item GOT entries consume data cache space.
  \item Lazy binding adds first-call overhead.
  \item Some optimizations (like function inlining across shared library boundaries) are inhibited.
\end{itemize}

Modern linkers mitigate these costs through:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Symbol preemption control}: \texttt{-Bsymbolic} prevents interposition, enabling direct calls.
  \item \textbf{IFUNC}: indirect functions resolved at load time for CPU-feature dispatch.
  \item \textbf{Relative relocations} (\texttt{RELRO}): read-only GOT segments after relocation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Link-Time Optimization}\label{sec:lto}

\subsection{The Whole-Program View}\label{sec:lto-whole-program}

Traditional compilation optimizes each translation unit independently. Link-time optimization (LTO) defers optimization until link time, when the whole program is visible.

\begin{definition}[Link-Time Optimization]\label{def:lto}
\emph{Link-time optimization} (LTO) is a compilation strategy where the compiler emits an intermediate representation (rather than machine code) in object files, and the linker performs cross-module optimization before generating final code.
\end{definition}

\subsection{LTO Implementation}\label{sec:lto-implementation}

The typical LTO workflow:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Each compilation unit produces an object file containing both machine code (for non-LTO fallback) and an IR summary (for LTO).
  \item The linker plugin reads all IR summaries and merges them.
  \item The compiler's optimizer runs on the merged IR, performing cross-module inlining, devirtualization, dead code elimination, and other optimizations.
  \item The optimized IR is compiled to machine code and linked.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{insight}
LTO enables optimizations that are impossible with separate compilation: cross-module inlining, whole-program dead code elimination, devirtualization of calls through known call targets, and global register allocation. The cost is increased build time and the need for the linker to understand the compiler's IR.
\end{insight}

\subsection{ThinLTO}\label{sec:thinline}

Full LTO requires merging all IR into a single module, which does not scale to very large programs. \emph{ThinLTO} (used by LLVM) addresses this by:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Computing summaries of each module's symbols and call graph.
  \item Performing cross-module analysis on summaries alone (import decisions, dead symbol detection).
  \item Importing only the functions that benefit from inlining, keeping modules largely separate.
  \item Compiling each module independently with the imported functions.
\end{enumerate}
ThinLTO achieves most of the benefit of full LTO with significantly better scalability.

\section{Binary Emission Strategies}\label{sec:emission-strategies}

\subsection{Direct Binary Emission}\label{sec:direct-emission}

The simplest emission strategy writes machine code bytes directly to the output file. The compiler's back end maintains a code emitter that translates each machine instruction into its binary encoding.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Advantages}: simple, fast, no external tool dependencies.
  \item \textbf{Disadvantages}: the compiler must implement the full encoding logic for each target.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Assembly Emission}\label{sec:asm-emission}

Many compilers emit textual assembly language and invoke an external assembler. This approach:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Advantages}: human-readable output, leverages the system assembler's encoding logic, easier to debug.
  \item \textbf{Disadvantages}: slower (requires parsing the assembly text), additional tool dependency, harder to do binary-level optimizations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{JIT Emission}\label{sec:jit-emission}

Just-in-time compilers emit code directly into executable memory at runtime:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Use OS APIs (\texttt{mmap} with \texttt{PROT\_EXEC}, \texttt{VirtualAlloc} with \texttt{PAGE\_EXECUTE}) to allocate executable memory.
  \item Encode instructions directly into the allocated buffer.
  \item Flush instruction caches after emission (required on architectures with separate I/D caches, like ARM).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Emission Libraries}\label{sec:emission-libraries}

Several libraries assist with binary emission:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{LLVM MC layer}: provides instruction encoding, assembly parsing, and object file emission.
  \item \textbf{Capstone/Keystone}: disassembly and assembly engines.
  \item \textbf{asmjit}: runtime x86/x64/AArch64 code generation library.
  \item \textbf{dynasm}: a dynamic assembler using Lua preprocessing.
\end{itemize}

\section{Debug Information Emission}\label{sec:debug-info-emission}

\subsection{The Need for Debug Information}\label{sec:debug-need}

Compiled code must be debuggable. Debug information maps machine instructions back to source locations, enabling debuggers to display source code, inspect variables, and set breakpoints. The compiler must emit this information alongside the code.

\begin{definition}[Debug Information]\label{def:debug-info}
\emph{Debug information} is metadata embedded in the object file that describes the correspondence between the compiled code and the source program. It includes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Source file names and line numbers.
  \item Variable locations (register, stack offset, or global address).
  \item Type information for variables and data structures.
  \item Inline function expansion records.
  \item Lexical scope information.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{DWARF Format}\label{sec:dwarf}

DWARF is the standard debug information format for ELF systems:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{.debug\_info}: the main debug information section, containing compilation units, subprograms, variables, and types organized as a tree of DIEs (Debugging Information Entries).
  \item \texttt{.debug\_line}: maps machine instruction addresses to source file and line number.
  \item \texttt{.debug\_abbrev}: defines the abbreviations used in \texttt{.debug\_info}.
  \item \texttt{.debug\_str}: string table for debug information.
  \item \texttt{.debug\_ranges}: non-contiguous address ranges for optimized code.
  \item \texttt{.debug\_loc}: variable location lists for optimized code (where a variable moves between registers and stack).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Debug Info and Optimization}\label{sec:debug-optimization}

Optimization complicates debug information:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Variables may be optimized away or stored in unexpected locations.
  \item Code may be reordered, making line number mappings non-monotonic.
  \item Inlined functions require nested scope records.
  \item The \texttt{.debug\_loc} section must track variable locations through register allocation and instruction scheduling.
\end{itemize}

The compiler must maintain debug information through all optimization passes, updating it as the IR changes. This is a significant engineering challenge, especially for aggressive optimizers.

\subsection{Split DWARF and Debug Fission}\label{sec:split-dwarf}

Debug information can be large (often larger than the code itself). Split DWARF (\texttt{.dwo} files) separates debug information from the object file:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The object file contains a small reference to the \texttt{.dwo} file.
  \item The \texttt{.dwo} file contains the full debug information.
  \item This reduces link time and object file size for non-debug builds.
  \item Debuggers load the \texttt{.dwo} file on demand.
\end{itemize}

\section{Code Size Optimization}\label{sec:code-size}

\subsection{The Code Size Problem}\label{sec:code-size-problem}

For embedded systems and mobile applications, code size is a critical constraint. The compiler must minimize the size of the generated code while maintaining correctness and acceptable performance.

\subsection{Code Size Techniques}\label{sec:code-size-techniques}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Function outlining}: extract common code sequences into shared functions.
  \item \textbf{Thumb/compact mode}: use compressed instruction encodings (ARM Thumb, RISC-V C extension).
  \item \textbf{Link-time dead code elimination}: remove unreferenced functions and data at link time.
  \item \textbf{String merging}: merge identical string literals.
  \item \textbf{ICF (Identical Code Folding)}: merge functions with identical machine code.
  \item \textbf{Section garbage collection}: remove unused sections (\texttt{--gc-sections}).
\end{itemize}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Keeping Section Groups During Linker Garbage Collection}

Section garbage collection is a graph traversal after symbol resolution, not a test for whether each section has a public name. An entry point can retain a local constant through a relocation. Likewise, some object formats or linker policies require a live section's companion sections to stay with it, even when no relocation directly names them.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Work on selected input sections after duplicate resolution, including selection among competing groups. Use roots determined by the output kind and linker policy: entry sections, required exports, retained initialization sections, or explicit script directives. The section graph includes resolved relocation targets and any other mandatory retention edges, such as group membership for groups that must be kept as a unit.

\begin{sudocode}[Mark the sections the output requires]
\Require Selected sections, roots $R$, retention edges
\Ensure Keep exactly the sections reachable under this policy
\State $L\gets\emptyset$; initialize worklist with $R$
\While{the worklist is not empty}
  \State Pop section $s$
  \If{$s\in L$}
    \State \textbf{continue} \Comment{Relocations can form cycles}
  \EndIf
  \State Add $s$ to $L$
  \State Queue local targets of live relocations from $s$
  \State Queue required companions of $s$
  \State \Comment{Unselected duplicates are not companions}
\EndWhile
\State Discard selected sections outside $L$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} The 24-byte entry section relocates to a 40-byte helper, which references an 8-byte constant. The helper also belongs to a selected group with a 12-byte companion. An unreferenced 16-byte section has a relocation back to entry. The linker keeps the first four sections (84 bytes before padding), and drops the 16-byte section. The backward relocation does not make its source live.

\textbf{Invariant.} Each marked section has a root-to-section retention path; every processed section queues all of its required successors. Marking takes linear time in the selected graph's size when adjacency lists are available. An undefined reference has no local section edge and must be handled by ordinary symbol and relocation policy. An omitted root, such as an initializer required by the ABI, can silently change program behavior.

\subsection{Size vs.\ Speed Trade-Offs}\label{sec:size-vs-speed}

Compilers typically offer optimization levels that trade code size for speed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{-Os} (GCC/Clang): optimize for size. Disables optimizations that increase code size.
  \item \texttt{-Oz} (Clang): aggressively optimize for size.
  \item \texttt{-O2}/\texttt{-O3}: optimize for speed, possibly at the cost of code size.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:code-emission-summary}

Code emission and linking transform the compiler's optimized machine code into executable artifacts. The object file format, relocation mechanism, and linking strategy all affect the final program's correctness, performance, and deployability. Modern compilers must handle multiple object formats, support both static and dynamic linking, generate position-independent code, and participate in link-time optimization---all while maintaining the performance and correctness guarantees that programmers expect.

\begin{historical}
The evolution of object file formats reflects the growing complexity of software systems. The original Unix \texttt{a.out} format was simple but inflexible. COFF (Common Object File Format) added sections and symbolic debugging. ELF, designed for System V Release 4, generalized COFF with support for dynamic linking, shared libraries, and multiple architectures. Meanwhile, Microsoft's PE format evolved from COFF through the needs of Windows, adding features like resource sections and manifest support. The modern trend toward WebAssembly and other portable formats represents the next evolution: binary formats designed for sandboxed execution across diverse platforms.
\end{historical}
